<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-ha-voice-command-dialog"><code>ha-voice-command-dialog</code></h3>

The dialog. People open it from the dashboard top bar or from a card that has the `assist` action, which accepts a `pipeline_id` and `start_listening`. `start_listening` is off by default on the web. Use it only when it is deliberate, and never start listening because of navigation or a theme change. The dialog remembers the pipeline people chose last.

**What the Civica theme sets.** The dialog surface uses the Civica surface colour (white in light mode and a dark surface in dark mode) with square corners, and the header text and divider use the Civica colours. On a small screen the dialog fills the screen.

**What stays as Home Assistant draws it.**

- The dialog width follows Home Assistant’s defaults, and the dimming behind it is not set by Civica.
- The pipeline menu button keeps its own height, so the theme’s 44 pixel button height does not apply to it.
- Header sizes are Home Assistant’s defaults.
- Card-mod is not supported for dialogs, so do not rely on it to change this one.

<h3 id="child-ha-assist-chat"><code>ha-assist-chat</code></h3>

The conversation and the input. It is also used in the more information dialog for a conversation entity.

- Pressing Enter in the field sends the typed request.
- The microphone button starts listening. While it records, a pulsing circle shows. If the browser cannot record, an alert icon shows and typing still works.
- Messages from Assist are written as Markdown. An error appears as its own bubble. Home Assistant can show a collapsible “thinking” section.

**What the Civica theme sets.** Text uses `primary-text-color`, and the input field takes the Civica form border, square corners and focus ring.

**What stays as Home Assistant draws it.**

- Bubble colours come from the primary colour for the person and the secondary background colour for Assist, and from the error colour for errors. Civica does not set the chat’s own bubble colours. Contrast follows the theme colours, so check it on your own dashboard in both modes. The error bubble uses dark text on a pale colour in dark mode.
- Bubble corners and the recording pulse keep Home Assistant’s rounded shapes.
- Do not rely on a screen reader announcing new messages. Always write the result in words.
- The pulse is an animation, so do not use it as the only sign that the dialog is listening.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- One dialog handles typed and spoken requests.
- Typing always works, even when speech does not.
- Civica does not change the microphone, the pipeline or Home Assistant’s answers.
- Bubble shape and colour are mostly outside the theme.
- The listening state is an animated icon, so say it in words too.
- A request can fail or be misunderstood. Plain wording in the error matters.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Keep the typed route available. Do not start the microphone without a clear choice. Say what Assist heard and what happened, in plain words, and never leave a result as colour or an icon alone. Give people time: there is no need to speak within a limit. Use the `assist` action on a card only with a clear label such as “Ask Assist”.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships and 1.3.2 Meaningful Sequence: messages are in order.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the listening and error states are written, not colour alone.
- 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum) and 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: check bubbles and icons on your own dashboard.
- 1.4.2 Audio Control: speech is under the person’s control.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard and 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap: the dialog holds focus by design and closes with Escape or the close button.
- 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide: the pulse loops while recording.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible, 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum) and 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum).
- 3.2.2 On Input: Enter sends only when people press it.
- 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions and 4.1.3 Status Messages.

A typed route, plain wording and no time limit keep the effort low.

